REASONING:
The enemy is expected to counter our river crossing with strong defensive forces. We will need to concentrate our force to overcome this resistance as well as provide security for our flanks.

To achieve this, we will conduct a pincer movement, pulling the enemy forces away from the river crossing and securing the bridgehead.

First, the blue-screen will move north to secure the northern flank and provide a screening element for our maneuver.

Second, the blue-support will move east and then south to approach the river from a flanking position.

Lastly, the blue-bridgehead will cross the river and attack the enemy forces at the river crossing.

DOCTRINE REFERENCE:
FM 3-90, 1-38, FM 100-5

